            "privateNotes": "",
            "publicNotes": "<p>While most nymphs bond with a natural feature, such as a tree, pool, or cave, horai are rare nymphs associated with a specific time—solstices and equinoxes—rather than a location. Horai erect simple astronomical calendars near their homes, which they use to mark the moment when the sun reaches its highest or lowest point in the sky. A hora tied to the summer solstice grows more powerful as the sun reaches its height, while becoming sickly and weak during the winter solstice. The opposite holds true for winter horai, who are weak in summer and strong in winter. Meanwhile, autumn and spring horai draw power near the autumnal and vernal equinoxes, respectively, becoming weaker around the opposite equinox.</p>\n<p>Summer horai appear constantly bathed in sunlight, with skin and clothing of red, orange, and yellow, like the sunlight of high summer.</p><h3>Winter, Spring and Autumn Horai</h3><p>The statistics here are for summer horai, but you can represent the other three types of horai with minor alterations.</p>\n<p><strong>Winter horai</strong> have resistance to cold rather than fire, and their strikes deal cold damage instead of fire; are winter bound (impeded during summer months); and have Winter's Rescue (+4 during the month of the winter solstice, +2 on the months just before and after, +1 in spring and autumn, and no benefit during the summer). For a winter hora queen, being tied to the land improves the environment in winter and twists it in summer.</p>\n<p><strong>Autumn horai</strong> have resistance to void instead of fire, and their strikes deal void damage instead of fire; are autumn bound (impeded during spring); and have Autumn's Rescue (+4 during the month of the autumnal equinox, +2 on the months just before and after, +1 in summer and winter, and no benefit during spring). For an autumn hora queen, tied to the land improves the environment in autumn and twists it in spring.</p>\n<p><strong>Spring horai</strong> have no resistance, and their strikes deal vitality damage instead of fire (harming only creatures with void healing, as normal); are spring bound (impeded during autumn); and have Spring's Rescue (+4 during the month of the vernal equinox, +2 on the months just before and after, +1 in winter and summer, and no benefit during autumn). For a spring hora queen, tied to the land improves the environment in spring and twists it in autumn.</p>",
            "publication": {
                "license": "ORC",
                "remaster": true,
                "title": "Pathfinder Feybound"
            }
        },
